00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:10.350
EVAN JONES: Hi, Evan here.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.601
I'm a technical curriculum
developer for Google Cloud.

00:00:12.601 --> 00:00:13.933
SANDRA SEQUEIRA: And I'm Sandra.

00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:15.570
I work on our cloud sales team.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.330
On this episode, we'll
introduce you to Cloud Datalab,

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.940
an interactive tool for
exploring, analyzing,

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.724
and visualizing large scale data
sets with just a few clicks.

00:00:23.724 --> 00:00:25.890
EVAN JONES: We'll also take
you through a quick demo

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.890
of a self-paced lab where
we'll create a Datalab instance

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.980
as well as create and
manage a new notebook.

00:00:36.392 --> 00:00:37.850
SANDRA SEQUEIRA:
Cloud Datalab aims

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.130
to help you get valuable
insights from raw data

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.200
while exploring,
publishing, and sharing

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.790
reports in a fast and
cost-effective way.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.630
EVAN JONES: Datalab is targeted
largely at data scientists.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.150
Creating Jupyter notebooks,
previously IPython,

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.130
has been a very popular
way for data scientists

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.390
to analyze data.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.730
So it's no coincidence that
Datalab is based on Jupyter

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.415
and is also open source.

00:00:57.415 --> 00:00:58.790
And what this
gives you is access

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.520
to a large set of open source
Python libraries for data

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.280
analysis, visualization, and
machine learning scenarios

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.530
that your fellow data
scientists have already written.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.655
What it means for you is
that the notebooks written

00:01:08.655 --> 00:01:10.220
by your peers can
easily be brought

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.495
into Datalab, which runs on
Google Cloud's infrastructure.

00:01:17.672 --> 00:01:19.130
SANDRA SEQUEIRA:
As Evan mentioned,

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.530
the real power of Datalab
comes from its integration

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:23.960
with other Google Cloud
Platform services.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.150
Datalab runs on
Google Compute Engine,

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.850
so you can choose a machine type
with the performance and cost

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.180
characteristics that suits
your data analysis needs.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.310
In terms of
scalability, analyzing

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:37.217
terabytes or petabytes of
data is no problem at all,

00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:39.800
because of the easy connections
to other Google Cloud big data

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:40.300
services.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.017
EVAN JONES: All right,
it's time for the Quiklabs.

00:01:47.017 --> 00:01:49.100
In this hands-on lab, which
takes about 30 minutes

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.980
to complete, we'll show you how
to create a Datalab instance

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:53.480
and create a new notebook.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.980
SANDRA SEQUEIRA:
We'll also show you

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.030
how to use unget in your browser
and get from the command line

00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.390
to manage your notebooks.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.090
You can check out the link
to start the Quiklab here.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.631
EVAN JONES: At this
point in the lab,

00:02:03.631 --> 00:02:06.260
we've created a Cloud Datalab
instance called my-datalab.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:14.500
We've used the web preview to
access the Datalab home page.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:25.300
We then created a new
notebook, ran the code,

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.040
and saved the notebook.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.260
Here, we're opening the
notebook's repository

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.060
using unget.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.310
We give the commit a title
and commit the new notebook

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:36.745
to the Cloud Datalab VM repo
and push to the master branch.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.650
To see the commit we made
using the command line,

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.140
we SSH into the
Datalab VM and open up

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.480
an interactive shell session
inside of the Cloud Datalab

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.468
container.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.800
We can navigate the notebook's
directory in the container

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.302
and see the get log.

00:02:54.302 --> 00:02:56.760
And this will include the commit
of our brand new notebook.

